{"flaws": [
  {"title": "Downward iterator skips the start tick when it is an initialized multiple of tick spacing, so active liquidity is wrong and rewards can be stolen",
   "severity": "High",
   "lines": "self.currentTick = startTick; ... (int16 wordPos,) = TickLib.position(startTick.compress(self.tickSpacing)); self.currentWord = self.manager.getPoolBitmapInfo(self.poolId, wordPos); _advanceToNextDown(self);",
   "sequence": "1. A pool has tickSpacing 10, a position over [0,30), and the current tick is exactly 30, the position's upper bound. The attacker can move the tick there with a one-for-zero swap if needed. Uniswap counts the [0,30) liquidity as inactive at tick 30.\n2. The attacker adds JIT liquidity L' to [20,30) and makes a top-of-block zero-for-one swap that lands just below 30.\n3. `afterSwap` calls `_zeroForOneDistributeTax`. `initDown` calls `reset(30, end)`. `reset` sets currentTick = 30 and then calls `_advanceToNextDown`, which searches from compress(30-1). Tick 30 is therefore never returned, although it must be crossed. Its net liquidity of -(L+L') is never subtracted, so that liquidity is never added to the running total.\n4. `CompensationPriceFinder.getZeroForOne` and `_zeroForOneCreditRewards` use a running liquidity that is too small. Later crossings either underflow in `liquidity.sub`, which reverts the swap, or leave a wrong remainder. The leftover tax is then credited to `globalGrowthX128` using the too-small liquidity.\n5. The growth inside the attacker's [20,30) range becomes about R/L'. The attacker removes liquidity and collects the whole reward balance R.",
   "impact": "LP rewards are misallocated and an attacker can take all accumulated rewards. In other states, zero-for-one top-of-block swaps revert because of the underflow.",
   "known_finding": "All rewards can be stolen due to incorrect active liquidity calculations when the current tick is an exact multiple of the tick spacing at the upper end of a liquidity range",
   "confidence": "high"}
 ],
 "requirement_breaks": ["When startTick is an initialized tick, reset does not make getNext return startTick itself, so starting from the active liquidity at startTick and subtracting each returned tick's net liquidity does not give the active liquidity at endTick (breaks requirements 4 and 5)."]}

